1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,190
Ask most product people what the EU AI Act actually says, and what do you get?

2
00:00:05,190 --> 00:00:09,967
Something vague about "regulating AI," which is roughly as useful as saying a

3
00:00:09,967 --> 00:00:15,117
nutrition label "regulates food." The actual document is far more specific, and far

4
00:00:15,117 --> 00:00:17,910
more checkable, than its reputation suggests.

5
00:00:17,910 --> 00:00:22,873
It sorts every AI system into one of four risk tiers, by what the system is used

6
00:00:22,873 --> 00:00:26,224
for, not by which model powers it or how it was built.

7
00:00:26,224 --> 00:00:31,436
Two features running the identical underlying model can land in completely different

8
00:00:31,436 --> 00:00:35,655
tiers, because the tiers are about the use case, not the technology.

9
00:00:35,655 --> 00:00:38,696
That's genuinely good news for most product work.

10
00:00:38,696 --> 00:00:42,543
A feature landing in the lightest tier faces close to nothing.

11
00:00:42,543 --> 00:00:47,072
A feature landing in the heaviest tier faces a real compliance programme.

12
00:00:47,072 --> 00:00:52,160
The entire job is knowing honestly which one you're in, which is what this lecture

13
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:53,215
actually teaches.

14
00:00:53,215 --> 00:00:57,496
This is also worth learning even if you never touch a single EU user.

15
00:00:57,496 --> 00:01:02,212
Plenty of regulation being drafted elsewhere borrows the same shape: sort by

16
00:01:02,212 --> 00:01:04,135
consequence, not by technology.

17
00:01:04,135 --> 00:01:09,223
Learn to read this one well and the next one is faster to read, not a fresh start.

18
00:01:09,773 --> 00:01:15,066
Read this table from the bottom up, because that's where most real product features

19
00:01:15,066 --> 00:01:15,959
actually land.

20
00:01:15,959 --> 00:01:21,188
Minimal risk covers an enormous amount of ordinary product work: internal drafting

21
00:01:21,188 --> 00:01:26,226
tools, search ranking, most copilots, anything that helps a person do their job

22
00:01:26,226 --> 00:01:29,542
without deciding something consequential about them.

23
00:01:29,542 --> 00:01:34,707
Limited risk adds exactly one obligation worth remembering: tell the user they're

24
00:01:34,707 --> 00:01:35,792
talking to an AI.

25
00:01:35,792 --> 00:01:40,829
If your feature already does that, and most chat-style features already should,

26
00:01:40,829 --> 00:01:44,528
you've likely cleared this tier without changing anything.

27
00:01:44,528 --> 00:01:49,757
High-risk is the tier worth actually stopping for, and it's defined by consequence

28
00:01:49,757 --> 00:01:51,989
to a person, not by sophistication.

29
00:01:51,989 --> 00:01:57,346
A hiring tool that screens candidates, a system that scores creditworthiness, a tool

30
00:01:57,346 --> 00:02:02,447
that affects access to education: these carry real obligations regardless of how

31
00:02:02,447 --> 00:02:04,488
simple the underlying prompt is.

32
00:02:04,488 --> 00:02:08,761
A basic classifier deciding who gets an interview can be high-risk.

33
00:02:08,761 --> 00:02:12,268
A frontier model drafting marketing copy usually isn't.

34
00:02:12,818 --> 00:02:17,734
The unacceptable tier is short and worth knowing by name rather than by detail,

35
00:02:17,734 --> 00:02:21,281
because almost no legitimate product feature lands there.

36
00:02:21,281 --> 00:02:26,321
It exists for practices most teams were never going to build anyway: manipulating

37
00:02:26,321 --> 00:02:31,548
people through subliminal techniques, scoring citizens for trustworthiness the way a

38
00:02:31,548 --> 00:02:33,975
small number of governments already do.

39
00:02:33,975 --> 00:02:39,015
If a feature idea makes you personally uneasy enough to wonder whether it belongs

40
00:02:39,015 --> 00:02:43,496
here, that discomfort is worth listening to before the tier confirms it.

41
00:02:43,496 --> 00:02:45,985
Tiers can also drift as a feature grows.

42
00:02:45,985 --> 00:02:50,527
A recommendation widget that only ever suggests products is minimal risk.

43
00:02:50,527 --> 00:02:55,568
The same widget, repurposed eighteen months later to influence which candidates a

44
00:02:55,568 --> 00:03:00,733
recruiter sees first, has quietly walked itself toward the high-risk tier without a

45
00:03:00,733 --> 00:03:03,346
single line of the original code changing.

46
00:03:03,346 --> 00:03:08,075
Re-check the tier when the use case changes, not only when the feature first

47
00:03:08,075 --> 00:03:08,635
launches.

48
00:03:09,185 --> 00:03:14,523
Notice the parallel to 8.3's training-data mistake, because it's the same shape of

49
00:03:14,523 --> 00:03:19,470
error: a comfortable assumption standing in for a check nobody actually ran.

50
00:03:19,470 --> 00:03:24,677
The Act is extraterritorial by design, the same way some privacy law already is.

51
00:03:24,677 --> 00:03:30,015
What matters is whether your feature's output reaches someone in the EU, not where

52
00:03:30,015 --> 00:03:33,660
your company is headquartered or where your servers sit.

53
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:38,672
A US-only company with EU customers, or even EU users of a nominally domestic

54
00:03:38,672 --> 00:03:44,010
product, can find the Act applies to that specific feature regardless of where the

55
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:44,660
team sits.

56
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:49,477
Check this deliberately, feature by feature, rather than assuming a single

57
00:03:49,477 --> 00:03:52,537
company-wide answer covers everything you ship.

58
00:03:52,537 --> 00:03:57,744
A feature with no EU exposure today can gain some the moment sales closes a deal

59
00:03:57,744 --> 00:04:03,147
nobody saw coming, which is exactly why this belongs as a dated, owned row in 8.1's

60
00:04:03,147 --> 00:04:07,052
register, not a one-time answer everyone forgets to revisit.

61
00:04:07,052 --> 00:04:12,129
Checking reach in practice is more concrete than it sounds: look at where your

62
00:04:12,129 --> 00:04:17,532
paying customers or registered users are actually located, not where your marketing

63
00:04:17,532 --> 00:04:18,378
targets them.

64
00:04:18,378 --> 00:04:23,846
A feature built for a domestic audience that quietly picks up EU-based users through

65
00:04:23,846 --> 00:04:28,988
an existing customer's global rollout has EU reach the moment those users start

66
00:04:28,988 --> 00:04:34,391
sending real requests, whether or not anyone updated a go-to-market document to say

67
00:04:34,391 --> 00:04:34,586
so.

68
00:04:35,136 --> 00:04:40,102
Say the honest caveat plainly, because regulation of this kind is being actively

69
00:04:40,102 --> 00:04:45,191
interpreted in real time, and a course can freeze a mental model far more reliably

70
00:04:45,191 --> 00:04:47,922
than it can freeze a set of legal specifics.

71
00:04:47,922 --> 00:04:53,074
What's stable is the shape: sort by use case, check reach rather than headquarters,

72
00:04:53,074 --> 00:04:58,102
treat consequence to a real person as the signal that pushes a feature toward the

73
00:04:58,102 --> 00:04:58,971
heavier tiers.

74
00:04:58,971 --> 00:05:04,122
What's not stable is exactly which use cases sit where, and exactly what a specific

75
00:05:04,122 --> 00:05:08,840
obligation requires in practice, both still being clarified as the Act's own

76
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:09,895
timeline unfolds.

77
00:05:09,895 --> 00:05:14,860
Bring the specific question to counsel, the same instruction as 8.3: not "are we

78
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:19,702
compliant," but "does this specific feature, used this specific way, for these

79
00:05:19,702 --> 00:05:24,791
specific users, fall into the high-risk tier." That's a question they can actually

80
00:05:24,791 --> 00:05:25,226
answer.

81
00:05:25,226 --> 00:05:29,943
Treat this the way 7.4 treated a model selection scorecard: a recheck, not a

82
00:05:29,943 --> 00:05:31,060
one-time decision.

83
00:05:31,060 --> 00:05:36,212
A feature that cleared the minimal-risk tier this year, under this year's guidance,

84
00:05:36,212 --> 00:05:41,178
is worth re-confirming on a cadence, the same discipline as everything else this

85
00:05:41,178 --> 00:05:43,412
section has asked of a register row.

86
00:05:43,962 --> 00:05:48,813
That's the check worth running honestly, and running it in that order matters: start

87
00:05:48,813 --> 00:05:53,607
from your most consequential feature, not your simplest one, because that's the one

88
00:05:53,607 --> 00:05:55,224
with something real to find.

89
00:05:55,224 --> 00:06:00,076
Most product portfolios have at most one or two features anywhere near the high-risk

90
00:06:00,076 --> 00:06:04,465
tier, and most people can name which ones they'd be, before ever opening the

91
00:06:04,465 --> 00:06:06,718
regulation, if they're honest about it.

92
00:06:06,718 --> 00:06:08,508
That instinct is usually right.

93
00:06:08,508 --> 00:06:13,360
Confirm it properly instead of leaving it as an unconfirmed hunch nobody wrote down.

94
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:16,767
Do this before a launch review asks you to, not during one.

95
00:06:16,767 --> 00:06:21,561
A tier assessment produced under deadline pressure, the week before a launch, tends

96
00:06:21,561 --> 00:06:24,102
to arrive at whatever answer keeps the date.

97
00:06:24,102 --> 00:06:28,434
The same assessment done two months earlier has room to actually change the

98
00:06:28,434 --> 00:06:32,361
feature's design if the honest answer turns out to be uncomfortable.

99
00:06:32,911 --> 00:06:34,798
One thing before you move on.

100
00:06:34,798 --> 00:06:39,807
Sort honestly, starting with whichever feature makes a decision that actually

101
00:06:39,807 --> 00:06:44,881
affects a person's access to something: a job, a loan, a place in a programme.

102
00:06:44,881 --> 00:06:49,955
Then check EU reach specifically for anything that lands in the top two tiers.

103
00:06:49,955 --> 00:06:55,355
If the answer is yes, or even genuinely uncertain, that's a row for 8.1's register,

104
00:06:55,355 --> 00:06:59,583
owned, dated, and headed for an actual conversation with counsel.

105
00:06:59,583 --> 00:07:04,917
Next lecture moves to the fifth row on 8.1's table: bias and fairness, scoped to a

106
00:07:04,917 --> 00:07:10,317
question you can actually check on your own feature instead of a topic too broad to

107
00:07:10,317 --> 00:07:10,772
act on.
